  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Jacob Lopez gave the A's a shutout framework and they made it stand. A 2-0 win is not a blowout, but when a starter limits Minnesota to one hit across five innings, survival becomes the game. The bullpen held for 3.3 innings without yielding a run. Hartlieb, Suarez, Alvarado, and Harris all stayed clean. And Tyler Soderstrom's single RBI in the fifth was enough. This was precision from the mound meeting just enough from the lineup.",
    "what_this_means": "They are a team that wins when the pitcher does the heavy lifting and the bats find one moment. Lopez struck out four and gave up nothing; that kind of line rarely loses, and this time it didn't. The offense was sparse. Five hits, five strikeouts. But Soderstrom's at-bat mattered because it came when runners were in position to score. One run from nine RISP situations is not an offensive statement, yet one run from a shutout is a complete game. The pattern holding here is not dominance; it's competence meeting opportunity exactly once when it counts. They've been inconsistent all season, and today the inconsistency worked in their favor instead of against them. Two runs is the bare minimum. The bar stays low because they keep finding ways across it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Minnesota brings TBD to the mound tonight. Mason Barnett takes the ball for Oakland at 4:10 PT. A chance to stack consecutive wins and pull within closer range of the division deficit.",
